{
  "subtype": "focus_inference",
  "implied_focus": "Travel-expense spend behavior and its anomalies/timing (amount magnitude, deviations, and processing/temporal context)",
  "per_column": [
    {
      "name": "spend_tier",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Buckets the amount into High/Medium/Low, directly framing spend magnitude as the central concept to analyze."
    },
    {
      "name": "is_amount_anomaly",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Flags outlier amounts, a natural analytical facet of a spend focus and directly actionable for auditing."
    },
    {
      "name": "processing_days",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Adds an operational/timeliness facet relevant to expense handling; loosely tied to spend but coherent as a process attribute."
    },
    {
      "name": "fiscal_quarter",
      "supports_focus": true,
      "rationale": "Temporal bucketing enabling spend trend analysis over periods; a coherent contextual facet of the spend focus."
    }
  ],
  "focus_coherence": 0.72,
  "structure_quality": 0.7,
  "focus_actionability": 0.8,
  "rationale": "The columns cohere reasonably around spend as the inferred focus: spend_tier and is_amount_anomaly both center squarely on expense amount (magnitude and outliers), while fiscal_quarter provides a temporal axis for trend analysis. processing_days introduces a distinct operational/timeliness dimension that is useful but slightly loosens the single-focus coherence, edging toward a two-theme set (spend vs. processing efficiency). Structurally the set forms a sensible predictor/attribute mix at useful granularity — tiers, a binary anomaly flag, a numeric latency, and a period label — rather than a bare restatement or noise. The implied focus (which expenses are large/anomalous and when) is clearly investigable and actionable for audit and budgeting, so actionability is high. Minor deductions reflect the empty descriptions and the mild dilution of a single coherent focus by the processing facet."
}

